>
<p>A burst pipe costs whatever the water touched. The pipe repair itself is usually the small part, often a few hundred dollars for a section of copper or PEX and a couple of hours of labor. The expensive part is everything downstream: drywall, insulation, flooring, cabinets, trim, and the drying and remediation work that has to happen before any of it gets rebuilt. A leak caught in ten minutes might total a few thousand dollars. The same pipe running unnoticed in a vacant unit over a long weekend regularly runs into the tens of thousands, and it can pass fifty thousand when it runs through two floors.</p>

<p>The prevention costs about the price of one dinner out. That gap is the entire reason this article exists.</p>

<h2>Why Middle Tennessee gets hit harder than the temperature suggests</h2>

<p>Places that freeze every winter build for it. Pipes run inside conditioned space, crawlspaces are sealed, and everyone knows the drill. Middle Tennessee has the worst of both worlds. We go weeks in the fifties, then drop into the teens for three days, and the housing stock was never built as though that mattered.</p>

<p>Four things in particular set us up.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Vented crawlspaces.</strong> A large share of the older housing here sits on a crawlspace with open foundation vents, which means the supply lines under the floor are in outdoor air with a floor over them.</li>
<li><strong>Exterior hose bibs.</strong> Especially on north and west walls, and especially with a garden hose still attached, which traps water at the valve.</li>
<li><strong>Garage and exterior wall runs.</strong> Laundry connections and kitchen lines on an exterior wall with thin insulation behind them.</li>
<li><strong>Attic runs in newer construction.</strong> Some builds run supply lines through the attic, which is the coldest part of the building envelope during a hard freeze.</li>
</ul>

<p>Add the fact that a freeze here usually arrives after a mild stretch, when nobody has thought about pipes in nine months, and you have the standard Middle Tennessee January.</p>

<h2>What the damage actually costs, and what drives the number</h2>

<p>Restoration pricing is driven by three variables, and none of them is the pipe.</p>

<p><strong>How long the water ran.</strong> A supply line under pressure can put out several gallons a minute. Ten minutes is a wet floor. Ten hours is a structural drying job. This is why an occupied home and a vacant unit are completely different financial events from the same failure.</p>

<p><strong>What it ran into.</strong> Water into an unfinished crawlspace or a garage slab is a drying problem. Water into finished space is a demolition and rebuild problem, because saturated drywall, insulation, and engineered flooring do not dry in place, they get removed and replaced.</p>

<p><strong>How many levels it crossed.</strong> A second floor failure that comes through a first floor ceiling doubles the affected area and adds the ceiling, the lighting, and often the electrical inspection.</p>

<p>Then there is the part that never shows on an invoice. On a rental, the unit is uninhabitable during remediation and rebuild, which is a vacancy you did not plan and a tenant you have to relocate or release. On a landlord policy, you also carry the deductible and the claim history. We put real numbers on what an empty day costs in <a href="/articles/cost-of-a-vacant-rental-day/">the cost of a vacant rental day</a>, and a freeze claim reliably produces a stack of them.</p>

<h2>The prevention list, in order of return</h2>

<p>Every item here is cheap, and the first three prevent most of what we actually see.</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Disconnect every garden hose and cover every hose bib.</strong> Foam covers cost a few dollars each. A hose left attached holds water against the valve and splits it, and the split is usually inside the wall where you do not find it until spring.</li>
<li><strong>Insulate exposed pipe in the crawlspace, garage, and attic.</strong> Foam sleeves are inexpensive per foot and take an afternoon. Insulate the whole run, not the easy part, because water freezes at the coldest inch.</li>
<li><strong>Close crawlspace vents before the first hard freeze, and check the crawlspace door actually latches.</strong> A door left open all winter turns the crawlspace into a wind tunnel.</li>
<li><strong>Seal air leaks near plumbing.</strong> Gaps around a rim joist penetration, a missing crawlspace vent cover, or an unsealed exterior wall penetration puts cold air directly onto a pipe.</li>
<li><strong>Know where the main shutoff is, and confirm it turns.</strong> Old gate valves seize. A shutoff you cannot turn during a failure is the same as no shutoff. Tag it and photograph it.</li>
<li><strong>On a vacant property, do not let the heat go off.</strong> Keep the thermostat at a minimum, open cabinet doors under sinks on exterior walls, and have someone lay eyes on it during a freeze. Vacant units are where the catastrophic losses happen, every time.</li>
<li><strong>Consider a water leak sensor or an automatic shutoff.</strong> Inexpensive sensors under water heaters, in laundry areas, and in crawlspaces are the cheapest early warning available.</li>
</ol>

<p>All of it belongs on a calendar rather than in your memory. It sits in the fall section of our <a href="/articles/annual-property-maintenance-checklist/">annual property maintenance checklist</a> for exactly that reason.</p>

<h2>If a pipe does burst</h2>

<p>The first two steps are yours, and they matter more than anything a contractor does later.</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Shut the water off at the main.</strong> Every minute is gallons.</li>
<li><strong>Kill power to the affected area at the breaker if water is anywhere near outlets, fixtures, or a panel.</strong></li>
<li>Open the faucets to drain the remaining pressure in the system.</li>
<li>Photograph everything before anything gets moved or torn out. Your insurer will want it and so will you.</li>
<li>Call your insurance carrier and start the claim. Water damage from a sudden burst is generally covered on standard policies, while damage from a long slow leak often is not, which is another argument for the sensors.</li>
<li>Get the water extracted and the space drying quickly. Mold does not need a week.</li>
</ol>

<p>Then the repair and rebuild get scheduled: the pipe section, insulation, drywall, paint, trim, and flooring. That is the phase we handle. To be straight about it, Upkeep does not run an emergency or on call service, so the shutoff and the immediate water extraction are on you or on a restoration company. What we do is the repair and put back work once the emergency part is over, on a flat quote with photo documentation, and the prevention work before it ever happens. Requests are confirmed within two business hours and routine work is scheduled within forty eight.</p>

<h2>For owners and property managers with more than one property</h2>

<p>The freeze prep list does not scale by memory. It scales by list. Before the first hard freeze, walk every property and record four things per address: hose bibs disconnected and covered, crawlspace vents closed and door secured, exposed pipe insulated, and main shutoff located and confirmed working. Note which units are vacant, because those get a second look during the freeze itself.</p>

<p>One crew running that list across a portfolio in the fall costs less than a single claim deductible. That is the whole argument. Our <a href="/services/plumbing/">plumbing services</a> page covers the repair side, our <a href="/property-managers/">property managers</a> page covers how we handle portfolio work orders and documentation, and if you want the seasonal walk on the schedule, send it through the <a href="/work-order/">service request form</a>.</p>

<p>Do it in October. Nobody has ever regretted covering a hose bib.</p>
